<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Percy Cox</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Percy Cox</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Percy Cox was a British army officer and colonial administrator who spent most of his career in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. As civil commissioner and then High Commissioner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1920 to 1923, he helped install Faisal I as king and set up the mandate government. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqair">Uqair</a> conference of 1922 he drew borders between Iraq, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/percy-cox.jpg" alt="Percy Cox" width="279" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, photo taken in 1916 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Percy_Cox_1916.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Percy Zachariah Cox was born on 20 November 1864 at Harwood Hall, Herongate, in Essex, one of seven children of the cricketer Arthur Zachariah Cox and Julienne Emily Cox. After Harrow School, as a third son with little inheritance, he entered the Royal Military College, Sandhurst, in February 1884 and was commissioned into the Cameronians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In 1889 he moved to the Bengal Staff Corps and on 14 November married Louisa Belle, daughter of the surgeon-general John Butler Hamilton.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After minor posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> he served in British Somaliland at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a>, and in 1895 defeated the Rer Hared clan, which had been raiding the coast, in six weeks with 52 regulars and 1,500 untrained irregulars. In October 1899 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> made him Political Agent and consul at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, where the French had leased a coaling station and protected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In June 1904 he became Acting Political Resident in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a>, and from 1906 urged India to change its policy towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>.</p><p>In the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> he served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> as Chief Political Officer and on 9 December 1914 took the formal surrender of Subhi Bey, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, ending the Battle of Qurna. He signed a treaty guaranteeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> £5,000 a month, was made honorary major-general in May 1917, and from November 1918 represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-agreement">Anglo-Persian Agreement</a> was concluded on 9 August 1919.</p><p>After the Iraqi Revolt of 1920 he became the first High Commissioner under the mandate, set up a largely Arab Council of State under the Naqib of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, Abd Al-Rahman Al-Gillani, and at the Cairo Conference of 1921 backed Faisal, whom he proclaimed king on 23 August 1921. When Faisal fell ill with appendicitis in August 1922, Cox took direct control, jailing opponents, silencing parties and newspapers and ordering the bombing of tribal insurgents. John Townsend wrote that it demonstrated British infallibility, illusory though it might have been; Ahmad Shikara called the measures severe and unpopular.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cox worked closely with Gertrude Bell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and each wrote of the other with admiration. His only son, Derek, was killed in action in 1917. After leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> he held no further government post and was president of the Royal Geographical Society from 1933 to 1936. He collapsed and died while hunting at Melchbourne, Bedfordshire, on 20 February 1937; the coroner recorded heart failure. He is considered one of the main architects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>'s present borders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Percy Cox” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British High Commissioners such as <b>Sir Percy Cox</b> advised the monarchy, while the Anglo-Iraqi treaties kept British control of foreign policy, finance and air bases.</blockquote><a href="/state/mandatory-iraq">Mandatory Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Herongate, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Melchbourne, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.59,0.36&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Herongate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4558 378 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Percy Cox"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5008 648h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5008 648h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.59,0.36&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
